---
/**
 * Diagram 2 — the tool-calling cycle.
 *
 * The one point this drawing exists to make: the *harness* executes the
 * tool, never the model. That is why the layout is a hub (harness) with two
 * satellites (model, tool) rather than a left-to-right pipeline — in a
 * pipeline the tool would sit downstream of the model, which is exactly the
 * wrong reading. Here the Model box has no edge to the Tool box at all; the
 * only line that reaches the tool starts at the harness, and it is the one
 * element in the diagram drawn in the jade accent.
 *
 * Theming and sizing: see StackDiagram.astro. Ids are prefixed `tc-` so they
 * cannot collide with the other two diagrams sharing this page.
 */
---

<figure class="qu-diagram">
	<svg
		viewBox="0 0 440 368"
		role="img"
		aria-labelledby="tc-title tc-desc"
		xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
	>
		<title id="tc-title">The tool-calling cycle: the harness runs the tool, not the model</title>
		<desc id="tc-desc">
			The harness sits at the center, with the model above it and a tool below
			it. Step one: the harness sends the model the prompt plus every tool
			definition, and each tool result on later turns. Step two: the model
			replies with text, or with a structured request to call a tool. Step
			three, highlighted: the harness runs the tool — the model cannot. Step
			four: the result goes back to the harness, which sends it to the model as
			the next turn, and the cycle repeats. The model and the tool are never
			connected to each other.
		</desc>

		<defs>
			<marker
				id="tc-arrow"
				markerWidth="9"
				markerHeight="7"
				refX="8"
				refY="3.5"
				orient="auto"
				markerUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
			>
				<path class="head" d="M0 0 L9 3.5 L0 7 Z" />
			</marker>
			<marker
				id="tc-arrow-accent"
				markerWidth="9"
				markerHeight="7"
				refX="8"
				refY="3.5"
				orient="auto"
				markerUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
			>
				<path class="head-accent" d="M0 0 L9 3.5 L0 7 Z" />
			</marker>
		</defs>

		<rect class="box" x="158" y="14" width="124" height="56" rx="8" />
		<text class="name mid" x="220" y="40">Model</text>
		<text class="role mid" x="220" y="58">asks, never runs</text>

		<rect class="box hub" x="144" y="152" width="152" height="64" rx="8" />
		<text class="name mid" x="220" y="180">Harness</text>
		<text class="role mid" x="220" y="198">the software you run</text>

		<rect class="box" x="158" y="298" width="124" height="56" rx="8" />
		<text class="name mid" x="220" y="324">Tool</text>
		<text class="role mid" x="220" y="342">read, edit, test</text>

		<line class="flow" x1="196" y1="146" x2="196" y2="78" marker-end="url(#tc-arrow)" />
		<text class="step end" x="134" y="96">1 · the prompt, plus</text>
		<text class="step end" x="134" y="112">every tool definition</text>
		<text class="role end" x="134" y="128">(and each tool result)</text>

		<line class="flow" x1="244" y1="74" x2="244" y2="146" marker-end="url(#tc-arrow)" />
		<text class="step start" x="306" y="102">2 · text, or a request</text>
		<text class="step start" x="306" y="118">to call a tool</text>

		<line class="flow accent" x1="196" y1="220" x2="196" y2="292" marker-end="url(#tc-arrow-accent)" />
		<text class="step end lead" x="134" y="240">3 · the harness</text>
		<text class="step end lead" x="134" y="256">runs the tool</text>
		<text class="role end" x="134" y="272">the model cannot</text>

		<line class="flow" x1="244" y1="294" x2="244" y2="222" marker-end="url(#tc-arrow)" />
		<text class="step start" x="306" y="250">4 · the result goes</text>
		<text class="step start" x="306" y="266">back to the harness</text>
	</svg>
	<figcaption>
		Step 4 feeds step 1 again, which is what makes it a loop. It stops when
		the model answers with plain text instead of another tool request.
	</figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
	.qu-diagram {
		margin: 2rem 0;
	}

	.qu-diagram svg {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 27.5rem;
		height: auto;
		margin-inline: auto;
		font-family: var(--sl-font);
	}

	.qu-diagram figcaption {
		margin: 0.875rem auto 0;
		max-width: 27.5rem;
		text-align: center;
		font-size: 0.875rem;
		line-height: 1.5;
		color: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
	}

	.box {
		fill: none;
		stroke: var(--sl-color-hairline);
		stroke-width: 1;
	}

	/* The hub gets the aside's surface tint so the eye lands on it first. */
	.hub {
		fill: var(--sl-color-bg-sidebar);
	}

	.flow {
		stroke: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
		stroke-width: 1.25;
		fill: none;
	}

	.head {
		fill: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
	}

	/*
	 * The only accented mark in the diagram, on the only claim readers get
	 * wrong: this arrow starts at the harness, not the model.
	 */
	.accent {
		stroke: var(--sl-color-accent);
		stroke-width: 1.5;
	}

	.head-accent {
		fill: var(--sl-color-accent);
	}

	/*
	 * Weight, not colour: the jade is spent on the arrow itself. Accent-
	 * coloured text inside the drawing would read as a link, since that is
	 * what accent text means everywhere else on the site.
	 */
	.lead {
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	.name {
		fill: var(--sl-color-white);
		font-size: 14px;
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	.step {
		fill: var(--sl-color-white);
		font-size: 12px;
	}

	.role {
		fill: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
		font-size: 11.5px;
	}

	.mid {
		text-anchor: middle;
	}

	.start {
		text-anchor: start;
	}

	.end {
		text-anchor: end;
	}
</style>
